id: attack-absorption
name: Attack Absorption
type: outOfSequence
actionCost: 0
kiCost: 0
attacking: false
requiresTarget: false
special: true
absorb: true
source: Core Rule
description: >
  Swallow an Energy or Magic attack your Parry turned aside. Your Opponent rolls its
  Wound Roll as if it had hit you, and half that Dice Score comes back to you as Ki -
  and if that is a fifth of your maximum, you may Power Up out of sequence.
text: >
  Attack Absorption Maneuver: You consume an enemy’s attack, taking its energy for
  your own.
  –Maneuver Type: Out-of-Sequence Maneuver
  –Action Cost: N/A
  –Minions: N/A
  –KP Cost: N/A
  –Effect: If you use the Parry option of the Defend Maneuver to successfully avoid an
  Attacking Maneuver of the Energy or Magic Foundation, you may absorb that Attacking
  Maneuver (meaning that Attacking Maneuver does not exist and therefore you cannot use
  the Reflect Maneuver in response to the successful Parry). Your Opponent makes an
  Urgent Wound Roll for their Attacking Maneuver as if you were hit, and you regain Ki
  Points equal to 1/2 of the Dice Score.

  If you regain Ki Points that equal or exceed 1/5 of your Maximum Ki Points through
  this effect, you may use the Power Up Maneuver as an Out-of-Sequence Maneuver.

---

# No script, for the reason every Out-of-Sequence Maneuver has none: it is never used
# from a list. It exists only as a chance something that just happened handed you, and
# that is the offer machinery rather than anything a script can reach. `absorb: true` is
# what hands it to the code, as `reflect` does for the Maneuver it sits beside.
#
# --- Where the offer comes from ---------------------------------------------------
#
# One door, and a narrower one than Reflect's: "the Parry option of the Defend Maneuver
# to successfully avoid an Attacking Maneuver". Reflect is offered from that door and
# from a won Might Clash on a Deflect as well; this entry names only the Parry, so only
# the Parry offers it.
#
# And no Area of Effect clause. Reflect's entry says "that did not possess an AoE" and
# this one does not, so an Area attack a Parry turned aside can be swallowed even though
# it could not be thrown back. Read from the two entries side by side rather than assumed
# to match, because they are one paragraph apart and differ.
#
# --- "That Attacking Maneuver does not exist" -------------------------------------
#
# Built as the sentence's own consequence and no further: "and therefore you cannot use
# the Reflect Maneuver in response to the successful Parry". That much is enforced, and
# it is enforced by a rule that was already there - one Out-of-Sequence Maneuver per
# character per card - so taking the Absorption closes the Reflect that was offered
# beside it without anything here having to say so.
#
# What it might mean for anybody else the same attack reached is left to the table. Two
# reasons. The sentence limits itself to your Parry and your Reflect; and an attack
# reaching four people is one Strike Roll and one Wound Roll in this system, so an
# absorption erasing it would be undoing Damage already settled against three people who
# had no part in this. If your table reads it the wide way, the attack is simply not
# applied to the rest - which is a ruling, and rulings are the table's.
#
# --- The Wound Roll ------------------------------------------------------------------
#
# "Your Opponent makes an Urgent Wound Roll for their Attacking Maneuver as if you were
# hit." Theirs, for their own attack - its Ki Wager, its Energy Charges, its Profile's
# own additions - which is exactly the Wound Roll the card already knows how to ask for.
# So nothing new is rolled: what changes is that an attack which hit nobody now owes one,
# and the button appears on the attacker's client the way it always does.
#
# It deals no Damage to you. You avoided it; the Parry won. "As if you were hit" is there
# to say there is a Wound Roll at all, and what it is for is the Ki.
#
# Urgent means it cannot be failed on purpose, which is the same flag a reflected
# attack's Wound Roll carries and the same thing Compelled does to a Combat Roll.
#
# --- The Ki ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
#
# "1/2 of the Dice Score" - the Dice Score is the whole roll, dice and bonuses together,
# and the half is rounded down as every half in these rules is.
#
# What you regain is what there was room for: three Ki short of full, you regain three
# whatever the roll came to. Which is also what the second paragraph measures, since "if
# you regain Ki Points that equal or exceed 1/5 of your Maximum" is about the Ki that
# actually came back. A character at full Ki absorbs an attack and gets no Power Up out
# of it, and that reading is deliberate.
#
# The fifth is asked by multiplying rather than dividing, so no rounding has to be
# invented: a fifth of 23 is 4.6, and 5 clears it where 4 does not.
#
# --- The Power Up ----------------------------------------------------------------------
#
# "You may use the Power Up Maneuver as an Out-of-Sequence Maneuver." Offered on a card of
# its own rather than on the attack, because the attack's one Out-of-Sequence Maneuver has
# just been taken - by this. That limit is right and this is not an exception to it: the
# Power Up is a second chance handed over by the absorption, not a second use of the
# absorption's own opening.
#
# Out of sequence waives the Action Cost and nothing else, so it is offered unpaid rather
# than free - Power Up's KP Cost is N/A, so that comes to the same thing here, and would
# not for anything else offered this way.
#
# Building this is what found that an offered Maneuver never ran its own script. The
# Out-of-Sequence door posted the card and stopped, so a Power Up handed over by an effect
# gained no stack of Power - and so did a Maneuver held by Triggered and released later.
# Both doors fire it now.
#
# --- What is not built ----------------------------------------------------------------
#
# "Minions: N/A." There are no Minions in this system - the Arrogant Declaration and
# Analysis files say the same about their own Minion lines. Carried as printed; what it
# buys is the table's.
